<main id="content"><header class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">Umicom Framework &amp; Applications</p><h1>Create windows, panels and controls</h1><p class="lead">Use shared component specifications in a practical Notes application.</p></header>
<h2>Describe the component before choosing its display</h2><p>A component specification says what a control represents: its kind, stable identifier, text, tooltip and layout preferences. The GTK adapter creates a widget from that description. Application state remains in the model; a button requests an operation rather than becoming the owner of a document or account.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Source location</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Component types and specification</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/include/umicom/ui/components/component.h"><code>include/umicom/ui/components/component.h</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>GTK factory interface</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/include/umicom/ui/gtk4/component_factory.h"><code>include/umicom/ui/gtk4/component_factory.h</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Runnable Notes window</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/examples/learning/projects/notes_window.c"><code>examples/learning/projects/notes_window.c</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Shared example model</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/examples/learning/projects/notes_model.c"><code>examples/learning/projects/notes_model.c</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Model interface</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/examples/learning/projects/notes_model.h"><code>examples/learning/projects/notes_model.h</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Build targets and tests</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/examples/learning/projects/CMakeLists.txt"><code>examples/learning/projects/CMakeLists.txt</code></a></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>Follow the existing Notes window</h2><p>The example combines a window, boxes, a split panel, labels, a text-entry control and an editor. Its File and Edit menus call the same Notes model operations as the other controls. The model delegates document work to Framework.</p><p>The helper below is taken directly from that example. It checks configuration before asking the GTK adapter to create a widget. This is a small part of the full program; compile the complete example directory, not this helper by itself.</p><pre><code>@UMICOM_SITE_COMPONENT_EXAMPLE@</code></pre><h2>What to notice</h2><p>The identifier is stable even when a displayed label changes. Tooltips and accessible names explain purpose to users. A label does not replace a field's validation rules. Layout containers decide how controls share the available space; resizing a panel must not reset the document behind it.</p><h2>Build the graphical example</h2><p>Use a checkout with the GTK4 development dependencies installed. The normal suite build can include the existing learning examples. Run each line separately and stop on a failure.</p><h3>Windows</h3><pre><code>Set-Location "C:\umicom\Umicom-Applications"
$env:Path = "C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin;$env:Path"
cmake --preset windows-ucrt64-all-debug -DUMICOM_BUILD_LEARNING_EXAMPLES=ON -DUMICOM_LEARNING_BUILD_GUI=ON
cmake --build --preset windows-ucrt64-all-debug --target umicom-learning-notes-window --parallel 2
&amp; ".\build\windows-ucrt64-all-debug\bin\umicom-learning-notes-window.exe"</code></pre><h3>Linux</h3><pre><code>cd "$HOME/umicom/Umicom-Applications"
cmake --preset linux-all-gtk4-debug -DUMICOM_BUILD_LEARNING_EXAMPLES=ON -DUMICOM_LEARNING_BUILD_GUI=ON
cmake --build --preset linux-all-gtk4-debug --target umicom-learning-notes-window --parallel 2
./build/linux-all-gtk4-debug/bin/umicom-learning-notes-window</code></pre><p>The target is declared in <code>framework/examples/learning/projects/CMakeLists.txt</code>. The source example requires its model and Framework dependencies; a headless build is not a way to validate its graphical behaviour.</p><h2>Try an application change</h2><p>Add a label explaining the save destination, then run the complete Notes example and resize its window. Check that the path, editor text and unsaved-work warning still behave correctly. Change a menu caption without changing its command identifier, and verify that its action is unchanged.</p><h2>Next components</h2><p>Use the <a href="../source-map.html">source map</a> to locate entries, lists, trees, grids, menus and layout contracts. A declared component kind does not establish that every adapter supports all its behaviours. Check the selected adapter, its tests and the actual native result before documenting an interaction as available.</p>
</main><footer><strong>Umicom Foundation</strong><p>Learn from the source. Build with shared components.</p><a href="../contributing.html">Improve these guides</a> · <a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation">GitHub organisation</a></footer></body></html>
